## What I did

#838 — closed. /read and /plan mode let git commit straight through. Your measurement was the whole issue, so there was nothing to diagnose — just the design question of which subcommands write. The answer is a pure git_write_subcommand(args) with a fail-closed allow list: 35 read-only subcommands enumerated, everything else treated as a write. Folded into detect_write_command rather than called separately, because I checked its non-test consumers and there is exactly one. Global options (git -C sub commit) and argument-dependent subcommands (config --get vs config x y) each needed explicit handling. Your near-miss guard is pinned at the emission point in both directions, and I deliberately did not reuse run_git's destructive list — it answers a narrower property and defaults the opposite way.

#839 — closed. Option 4 composed with option 1's tier: scope_limit is now parsed, PartiallyGraded is a real state, PartiallyStudied sorts between NeverStudied and VisitedUngraded. Both consumers wired, precedence rule stated once and read by both.

## The part worth flagging

I ran the command your issue said was un-run, and the result is not the satisfying one. src/safety.rs doesn't appear in yoyo risk epistemic at all — compute_epistemic_ranking only pushes entries scoring > 0.0, and study weights are negative discounts, so a file whose only signal is -0.75 is excluded outright. The tier is correct and tested; it's currently unobservable on the exact file that motivated it.

An earlier draft of my notes asserted the flattering version before I ran it. Catching that in my own writing is the reason I went and ran it.

## What I skipped, and why

#810, #794, #835, #833, #830, #801, #738, #683 — I touched none of their code this session. The file list confirms it rather than my memory. #834 was on the do-not-repeat list.

Both fixes ship with their limits stated rather than implied: #838 is a subcommand classifier, not a git parser (a gitconfig alias is invisible to it), and #839 makes whole-file over-crediting nameable, not impossible — a round that declares no scope is still credited whole-file. That was always the ceiling on option 4; better to say so in the closing comment than let the close imply more.
